1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,360
All right, so what this means is if x is 1,

2
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:09,760
then it is selling you insurance at the actuarially fair rate.

3
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:14,960
So that means that there's a 40% chance that I have to pay.

4
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:19,480
So I'm going to have to pay 40% of the amount of dollars

5
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:20,880
that I will get in this event.

6
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:26,520
Now, according to normal common sense morality,

7
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:28,240
is it fair to expect companies to sell

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:30,480
your insurance at the actuarially fair rate?

9
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:30,980
No.

10
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:33,520
Is actuarially fair insurance fair?

11
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:34,240
No.

12
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:35,880
No, well, what's unfair about it?

13
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:37,380
It's expecting the insurance company

14
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:38,340
to give you things for free.

15
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:40,080
Yeah, it's expecting the insurance company

16
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:43,240
to work through your slaves, to do everything for free.

17
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:46,280
So you would never expect them to actually do that.

18
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:48,760
But it's still a useful starting point

19
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:50,160
just to see what that would mean.

20
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:52,600
And then you can go and vary that markup.

21
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,960
So anyway, so you'd expect that x would always

22
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:56,720
be greater than 1 in the real world

23
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:00,080
because they need to have some way of covering their overhead.

24
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,680
There's overhead for insurance companies,

25
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:05,600
different employees, people.

26
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:08,520
They need someone to develop a phone tree that

27
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:12,160
disconnects you after taking different steps.

28
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:14,160
So that doesn't happen on its own.

29
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:16,160
It takes work.

30
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:19,280
So that's where it goes.

31
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:21,960
Now, this person here that has an expected utility

32
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:25,200
problem to solve, he's going to maximize with respect

33
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:28,920
to i.

34
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:31,960
So he's trying to decide how many dollars of insurance

35
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:33,280
to purchase.

36
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:36,480
And he's going to be maximizing this expected utility function.

37
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:42,280
So 60% chance of $1,000 minus the premium.

38
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:44,400
Now, the charming thing about insurance premiums,

39
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:48,920
suppose that everything works out great.

40
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:52,160
Do you still have to pay your insurance premium for that year?

41
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:54,120
Sure.

42
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:57,160
So the whole idea of the premium is that whether or not

43
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:00,280
you actually collect, you still pay the premium.

44
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:02,360
So you're going to subtract out the premium, which

45
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:04,800
will be 0.4x times i.

46
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,800
Then, the utility of that outcome

47
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:10,040
will be raised to the 0.8 power because that's

48
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:13,240
what your utility wealth function looks like.

49
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,640
And then there's a 40% chance of the other thing happening.

50
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:20,000
The other thing happening, you get $0 of market income,

51
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,600
and you still pay the policy.

52
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:25,480
So you also have to pay the premium even in bad years.

53
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:26,840
Good years, bad years, you always

54
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:28,880
write the check for the premium.

55
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:31,720
But the good news is in the bad state of affairs,

56
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:34,840
the insurance company gives you i dollars.

57
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:40,880
So you bought i dollars at a cost of 0.4 times x times i,

58
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:48,920
where x is whatever the market for overhead.

59
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:53,920
And then we need to raise that to the 0.8 power.

60
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:59,120
So this is a bit messy, but nothing

61
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:00,960
that you haven't encountered before.

62
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:04,760
So if we just simplify it, and then we differentiate it,

63
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:06,840
and then we set it all equal to 0,

64
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:10,640
then we will get the mess at the top of the next page.

65
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:15,840
So you get 0.6 times 0.8 times negative 0.4x times

66
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:19,320
that thing inside raised to the negative 0.2 power.

67
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:22,920
Everyone remember the following negative 0.2 powers.

68
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:28,800
And then plus 0.4 times 0.8 times 1 minus 0.4x times

69
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:31,040
all that mess also raised to that.

70
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:33,160
You also raised to the negative 0.2 power.

71
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:35,320
That equals 0.

72
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:37,000
Now, if you're getting very nervous,

73
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,360
the good news is if you've got something

74
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,800
raised to a weird power plus another thing raised

75
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:44,400
to a weird power all equal to 0, you can generally

76
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:47,480
go and separate it out and then rearrange terms.

77
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,520
And then you just get one expression

78
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:53,160
raised to that weird negative 0.2 power.

79
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:57,160
So if you do, you would see that in line E.

80
00:03:57,160 --> 00:03:59,880
And then all you have to do is take the negative 0.2

81
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:01,760
root of both sides.

82
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:04,280
Remember when's the last time you took the negative 0.2

83
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,720
root of something?

84
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:07,400
Like I said, it's probably been a while.

85
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:08,240
But it can be done.

86
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:08,860
You're familiar.

87
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:11,000
You remember how these things are all possible.

88
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,880
You can take any kind of odd root that you want.

89
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:16,000
It's all doable.

90
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,200
And then if we go and define theta

91
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:23,280
as being that mess there, all raised to the negative 0.5

92
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:26,840
power, we get the answer.

93
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:35,520
So we get I star, which is the optimal amount of dollars

94
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:41,480
insurance to buy, will be equal to 1,000 all over theta times

95
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:46,760
1 minus 0.4x plus 0.4x.

96
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:48,120
All right.

97
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:51,000
Now, at this point, this probably still

98
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,040
seems like it isn't really telling you anything

99
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:53,540
interesting.

100
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:55,680
And especially this hour a day, you

101
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:58,040
may be baffled by the math or what in the world

102
00:04:58,040 --> 00:04:59,360
is going on here.

103
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:01,520
But here is something interesting,

104
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:06,240
which is that if the insurance really is actually fair,

105
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:08,560
so x equals 1, then that theta thing

106
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:11,280
simplifies down very nicely.

107
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:12,760
So in fact, theta then becomes.

108
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:16,480
So if x is 1, then theta is equal to 1.

109
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:18,840
And if theta is 1, then look at how nicely everything

110
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:20,160
simplifies.

111
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:21,680
Because then we've got 1.

112
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:22,480
So we have 1.

113
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:23,240
So that's 1.

114
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:27,000
Then this can be 1 minus 0.4x plus 0.4x.

115
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,640
The 0.4x is canceled.

116
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:31,600
That leaves 1 in the denominator.

117
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:35,040
And then I star is going to just be equal to 1,000.

118
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:38,520
So this says that if there's actuarially fair insurance,

119
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:41,880
then a risk averse person will fully insure.

120
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:47,040
They will buy $1,000 worth of insurance at a cost of $400.

121
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:51,280
And then they will have $600 regardless

122
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:53,360
of the state of the world.

123
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:55,780
And that's exactly what you'd expect a risk averse person

124
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:58,600
to do if there's actually a fair insurance around.

125
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:00,960
Because basically, what is actually a fair insurance?

126
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:03,520
It is an opportunity to get the average income

127
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:06,360
at every possible state of the world.

128
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:09,440
And if you're risk averse, isn't that exactly what you would want?

129
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:09,940
Of course.

130
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:11,480
So that makes sense.

131
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:14,040
And then as you increase the markup, as you might guess,

132
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:16,200
this means that you'll buy less than that insurance.

133
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:17,960
So even though you're risk averse,

134
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:20,400
as the price of insurance goes up,

135
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:24,680
you are willing to tolerate more and more risk.

136
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:27,480
Make sense?

137
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:30,640
And then if x were to get really high,

138
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:34,920
even risk averse people wouldn't want to buy any insurance.

139
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:36,180
Wouldn't want to buy insurance at all.

140
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:40,480
And instead, they would go and sell insurance to others.

141
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:44,560
Who would risk averse people sell insurance to?

142
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:46,200
People who are?

143
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:47,480
More risk averse?

144
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:50,800
Well, even more risk averse.

145
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:54,720
Yes, so the people that have w raised to the 0.8 utility

146
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:57,280
function might sell insurance to the people with a w raised

147
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:59,440
to the 0.7 utility function.

148
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:01,920
Or w raised to the 0.1.

149
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:04,840
So it is possible, then, you could have insurance markets

150
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:07,080
in a world where everyone's risk averse.

151
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:08,600
Just like in a world where everyone

152
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:10,080
hates the smell of garbage, there's

153
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:12,000
still going to be some garbage men.

154
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:13,500
And who will the garbage men generally

155
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:15,920
be in the world when everyone hates the smell of garbage?

156
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:17,080
People who hate it the least.

157
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:18,800
Yeah, people who hate it the least.

158
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:23,160
So it's a nice intuitive result.

159
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:25,600
Now, in an economist model, the economy overall,

160
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:26,920
it's very common to say, well, let's

161
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:28,480
assume that consumers are risk averse

162
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:30,140
but firms are risk neutral.

163
00:07:30,140 --> 00:07:31,520
Although, when people actually go

164
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:33,060
try to study what firms do, it seems

165
00:07:33,060 --> 00:07:35,560
that firms also act as if they are risk averse.

166
00:07:35,560 --> 00:07:39,280
Possibly because firms are run by humans.

167
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:41,840
Interesting possibility.

168
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:44,200
Although, you may have also heard there are cases where

169
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:46,080
firms seem to be risk averse, risk aferring,

170
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:48,360
or act as if they are risk aferring.

171
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:52,840
Like, have you ever heard of zombie whatevers,

172
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:55,240
like zombie SNLs?

173
00:07:55,240 --> 00:07:56,960
But again, normally, like I was saying,

174
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:59,320
have you ever heard this expression, zombie SNLs?

175
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:03,040
Basically, if you've got a firm where you're already

176
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:04,760
like you're allowed to keep doing business

177
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:07,800
even though you have negative net worth.

178
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:11,720
So this is a situation where if you just accept the average,

179
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:13,720
then you lose money for sure.

180
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:16,040
Whereas, if you do a gamble, then either you

181
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:19,160
will lose even more money, but there's limited liability,

182
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:20,880
so no harm done.

183
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:24,360
Or you will get lucky and then things will work out for you

184
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:26,080
and then you will be back in the clear.

185
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:28,840
So, anybody else?

186
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:30,320
All right, so questions.

187
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:33,080
Questions about insurance?

188
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:34,400
Happy with insurance?

189
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:36,680
Anyone want to start solving problems like this?

190
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:38,760
If you're itching, as soon as class lights out,

191
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:41,720
you will be solving insurance problems.

192
00:08:41,720 --> 00:08:44,760
That's the basic idea.

193
00:08:44,760 --> 00:08:51,160
Now, last topic for symmetric and perfect information.

194
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,520
So there are a lot of textbooks that

195
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:56,720
give a list of conditions under which markets fail.

196
00:08:56,720 --> 00:08:59,880
And a standard condition of market failure

197
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:02,480
that you will see in many textbooks to this day

198
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:04,520
is imperfect information.

199
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:08,280
Information is imperfect and therefore markets fail.

200
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:10,320
This turns out to be just a gross overstatement.

201
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:12,600
It's just wrong.

202
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:15,360
Because it's totally possible to combine market efficiency

203
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:19,240
with symmetric and perfect information.

204
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:21,520
And actually, as we're going to see,

205
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:23,440
even when it's asymmetric, it might work out.

206
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:25,680
But when it's symmetric and perfect information,

207
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:28,480
and again, remember, that's when everyone is equally in the dark.

208
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:32,360
There's no one who knows better than anybody else.

209
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:34,280
And then it's very easy to show cases

210
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:37,320
where, even though people have imperfect information,

211
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:39,080
still everyone is acting optimally

212
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:42,320
and there is no failure of any kind.

213
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:44,720
So let's just go through some examples.

214
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:46,360
So suppose I don't know how much I'm

215
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:49,080
going to enjoy my consumption bundle.

216
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:50,920
So I might have my utility function

217
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:55,200
is going to be x to the a times y to the 1 minus a plus

218
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:56,320
epsilon.

219
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:59,720
Epsilon is normally distributed with mean 0.

220
00:09:59,720 --> 00:10:03,120
And what's my optimal decision?

221
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:05,160
My optimal decision is exactly the same

222
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:08,880
as if the epsilon were 0 all the time.

223
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:11,480
So basically, what's happening here is you optimize.

224
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:13,940
And then the universe just gives you either better or worse

225
00:10:13,940 --> 00:10:16,080
utility regardless of what you did.

226
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:18,240
So in that case, there's no reason

227
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:20,360
to think that there's any change.

228
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:22,360
And there is no change in front of the outcome.

229
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:24,320
So if the outcome is optimal before,

230
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:27,560
then despite the imperfect information, remains optimal.

231
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:30,920
Or it could be that suppose you don't know your relative taste

232
00:10:30,920 --> 00:10:32,280
for x and y.

233
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:33,200
So this one's more fun.

234
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:36,080
So suppose that you're maximizing, again,

235
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:41,000
so you've got utility of x and y.

236
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,120
And it's going to be given by x raised to the a,

237
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:45,280
y raised to the 1 minus a.

238
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:47,280
But then, what is a?

239
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:49,960
Well, a could actually be unknown.

240
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:54,840
So it could be that a equals 0.5 with a probability of 0.6.

241
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:59,920
Or a could equal 0.9 with a probability of 0.4.

242
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:01,760
And you're dealing with that.

243
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:04,760
And again, then what's the optimal thing to do?

244
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:08,000
The optimal thing to do is say, well, 60% chance

245
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,920
we're in this world, 40% chance we're in that world.

246
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:14,240
And I'll just go and do expect utility.

247
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:19,080
And the fact that I am deeply confused about what I like

248
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:21,240
doesn't lead to a suboptimal outcome.

249
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:24,800
It leads to the optimal outcome.

250
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:27,840
Now, this is only true if it's a scenario where, in this case,

251
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:29,320
this needs to be symmetric.

252
00:11:29,320 --> 00:11:31,480
So now suppose that this is what I thought,

253
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:33,560
but then the government knew, no, no, no.

254
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:35,920
This is what your preference is for sure.

255
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:37,040
I just know.

256
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:37,720
You're an idiot.

257
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:38,600
I know.

258
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:40,040
In that case, then the government

259
00:11:40,040 --> 00:11:42,800
might be able to help you because it's not symmetric

260
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:43,840
anymore.

261
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:46,960
But if no one knows any more than you do,

262
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:49,360
then there's no way of actually proving upon this outcome.

263
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:55,200
And if you go to Robin Hanson, he'll even say,

264
00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:56,440
no, he wouldn't tell you, sir.

265
00:11:56,440 --> 00:11:59,160
There are theorems saying that as long as the information is

266
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:02,320
truly symmetric, and again, that includes symmetric not just

267
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:05,360
for market actors, but for all creatures.

268
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:07,160
It's got to be symmetric for all creatures,

269
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:08,640
including the government.

270
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:11,880
But as long as it is symmetric, then there

271
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:13,960
is no reason to think that the market outcome is

272
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:16,680
any worse than normal.

273
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:18,720
So it's just not true that a perfect information

274
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:20,360
in general causes market failure.

275
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:25,360
So the more general, so intuitively, here's

276
00:12:25,360 --> 00:12:28,160
the thing is, just because you're ignorant

277
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:29,560
doesn't mean you're stupid.

278
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:31,880
So if you're in this situation, you could either say, my god,

279
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:33,760
I don't know what my own utility function is.

